  "text": "**Solution 1 is wrong.** Its argument gives a lower bound, h(A) ≥ c·n^{2/3}, but a lower bound on g(n) cannot show that g(n)/n → 0. That would need an upper bound of o(n), which is never supplied. The conclusion also conflicts with a standard fact: a 3-uniform hypergraph with average degree at most 3 has independence number linear in n (Spencer-type bounds). So the 3-AP structure of a weak Sidon set forces g(n) = Θ(n), and the limit cannot be 0.\n\n**Solution 2 is plausible but not verified.** The reduction is sound: a Sidon subset of a weak Sidon set is exactly a 3-AP-free subset. The Fibonacci-type example checks out at n = 5: the APs are (0,1,2), (0,2,4), (1,4,7), and the largest AP-free subset has size 3. The chain structure (j, j+2, j+3) also appears to admit density 3/5 (for example, the period-5 pattern 1,1,0,0,1 avoids every forbidden triple). So this construction gives g(n) ≤ (3/5 + o(1))n.\n\nThe gap is the matching lower bound. The \"LP bound\" of 2n/3 is only a fractional relaxation, and the claim that 3/5 is tight for every weak Sidon set is asserted, not proved. I cannot confirm that 3/5 is the true minimum, so this answer is unverified rather than established.\n\nBetween the two, Solution 2 is the more credible candidate, though its final value is not confirmed.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
